{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778517589123,"sessionID":"ses_1e8183fceffenG367CcnNzvwcT","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a858ab22642b6b1b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"conventional dendritic cell"},"output":"Found 193 matches (showing first 100)\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json:\n  Line 2:   \"body\": \"**CL term**\\n[CL_0000999](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000999) — CD4-positive CD11b-positive dendritic cell\\n\\n**Suggested revision of class hierarchy**\\nChange the Equivalent To class from `conventional dendritic cell` to [CD11b-positive dendritic cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002465).\\n\\n**Rationale**  \\nCurrently, CD4-positive CD11b-positive dendritic cell (CL_0000999) has `conventional dendritic cell` as its Equivalent To class. We propose changing it to CD11b-positive dendritic cell. This change reflects a more natural hierarchical relationship, as CD11b-positive dendritic cell represents a parent concept encompassing CD4-positive CD11b-positive dendritic cell.\\n\\nA concrete example supporting this change is already present in the ontology: CD4-negative, CD8-alpha-negative, CD11b-positive dendritic cell (CL_0002454) uses CD11b-positive dendritic cell as its Equivalent To class. Applying the same principle here ensures consistency and aligns with existing ontology modeling practices.\\n\\nThank you for your consideration.\\n\",\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/templates/blood_and_immune_upper_slim.csv:\n  Line 29: CL:0000990,http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/cl#blood_and_immune_upper_slim,conventional dendritic cell\r\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/definitions.owl:\n  Line 982: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1016/bs.ircmb.2019.07.004\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1016/j.immuni.2007.06.006\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1016/j.immuni.2018.12.027\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1038/nri3865\") <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000784> \"Plasmacytoid dendritic cells (pDCs), initially known as interferon-producing cells, are a unique subpopulation of dendritic cells. Unlike conventional dendritic cells (cDCs), plasmacytoid dendritic cells are characterized by their distinctive plasma cell-like morphology. These cells play vital roles in the innate and adaptive immune response, particularly in the antiviral response. Highly prevalent in the blood and lymphoid organs, pDCs are rare in non-lymphoid tissues, with the exception of inflamed tissues and tumors where they are often recruited.\n\n  Line 1108: In addition to their role in antigen presentation, cDCs have other important functions in maintaining immune homeostasis. They can perceive signals from the surrounding tissue microenvironment, responding to inflammation, and influencing or even polarizing T cell differentiation into various effector subtypes. Importantly, while cDCs are often associated with immunogenic responses, they also play a crucial role in immune tolerance, particularly towards self-antigens, thereby preventing autoimmune reactions. In conclusion, conventional dendritic cells serve as the sentinels of the immune system, monitoring for threat signals and coordinating the appropriate immune response.\n\n  Line 1111: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:label \"conventional dendritic cell on CELLxGENE CellGuide\") rdfs:seeAlso <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000990> \"https://cellxgene.cziscience.com/cellguide/CL_0000990\")\n\n  Line 1155: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1002/embj.201488027\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1084/jem.20161135\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1111/imm.12888\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1146/annurev-immunol-061020-053707\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:\\\\10.1038/s41423-021-00726-4\") <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002010> \"The pre-conventional dendritic cell (pre-cDC) are precursors of mature dendritic cells. They develop from common DC precursors in the bone marrow and exist in a semi-differentiated state. The term 'pre-conventional' denotes the stage of development of these cells prior to their maturation into conventional dendritic cells, which are critical players in immune responses. \n\n  Line 1156: After leaving the bone marrow, pre-cDCs transiently circulate in the bloodstream to colonize lymphoid and non-lymphoid tissues, including skin, mucosa, spleen, and lymph nodes where they differentiate into immature conventional dendritic cells, which undergo further differentiation. In mouse and humans, the population of pre-cDCs has been shown to be heterogeneous with two subpopulations that pre-committed to differentiate into different subsets of dendritic cells (cDC1 and cDC2). \n\n  Line 1158: The main function of pre-conventional dendritic cells is to constantly replenish conventional dendritic cells. In response to infection additional pre-cDCs are recruited to the site of infection to meet increased tissue demand, and this pre-DC recruitment may be necessary for effective T cell responses. The molecular signals that direct pre-cDC migration remain poorly understood but recent research suggest that it involves specific chemokine receptors expressed on pre-cDCs.\n\n  Line 1161: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:label \"pre-conventional dendritic cell on CELLxGENE CellGuide\") rdfs:seeAlso <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002010> \"https://cellxgene.cziscience.com/cellguide/CL_0002010\")\n\n  Line 1447: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1007/s11427-013-4482-4\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.4049/jimmunol.2100692\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.7554/eLife.60188\") <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002365> \"Medullary thymic epithelial cells (mTECs) are distinct cells situated in the thymic medulla, a vital organ where T cells mature. mTECs are recognized by their unique appearance, characterized by large, plump cells with abundant cytoplasm and irregularly shaped nuclei. They inhabit a microenvironment closely interacting with various thymic cell types, including conventional dendritic cells and thymocytes.\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/data/default/ExtendedDescription.tsv:\n  Line 1071: CL:0002365\tCL:0002365\tCL_0002365\t\"Medullary thymic epithelial cells (mTECs) are distinct cells situated in the thymic medulla, a vital organ where T cells mature. mTECs are recognized by their unique appearance, characterized by large, plump cells with abundant cytoplasm and irregularly shaped nuclei. They inhabit a microenvironment closely interacting with various thymic cell types, including conventional dendritic cells and thymocytes.\n\n  Line 1522: CL:0000784\tCL:0000784\tCL_0000784\t\"Plasmacytoid dendritic cells (pDCs), initially known as interferon-producing cells, are a unique subpopulation of dendritic cells. Unlike conventional dendritic cells (cDCs), plasmacytoid dendritic cells are characterized by their distinctive plasma cell-like morphology. These cells play vital roles in the innate and adaptive immune response, particularly in the antiviral response. Highly prevalent in the blood and lymphoid organs, pDCs are rare in non-lymphoid tissues, with the exception of inflamed tissues and tumors where they are often recruited.\n\n  Line 1637: In addition to their role in antigen presentation, cDCs have other important functions in maintaining immune homeostasis. They can perceive signals from the surrounding tissue microenvironment, responding to inflammation, and influencing or even polarizing T cell differentiation into various effector subtypes. Importantly, while cDCs are often associated with immunogenic responses, they also play a crucial role in immune tolerance, particularly towards self-antigens, thereby preventing autoimmune reactions. In conclusion, conventional dendritic cells serve as the sentinels of the immune system, monitoring for threat signals and coordinating the appropriate immune response.\"\tDOI:10.1016/bs.ircmb.2019.07.004|DOI:10.1146/annurev-immunol-061020-053707|DOI:10.3389/fimmu.2018.03176\n\n  Line 1663: CL:0002010\tCL:0002010\tCL_0002010\t\"The pre-conventional dendritic cell (pre-cDC) are precursors of mature dendritic cells. They develop from common DC precursors in the bone marrow and exist in a semi-differentiated state. The term 'pre-conventional' denotes the stage of development of these cells prior to their maturation into conventional dendritic cells, which are critical players in immune responses. \n\n  Line 1665: After leaving the bone marrow, pre-cDCs transiently circulate in the bloodstream to colonize lymphoid and non-lymphoid tissues, including skin, mucosa, spleen, and lymph nodes where they differentiate into immature conventional dendritic cells, which undergo further differentiation. In mouse and humans, the population of pre-cDCs has been shown to be heterogeneous with two subpopulations that pre-committed to differentiate into different subsets of dendritic cells (cDC1 and cDC2). \n\n  Line 1669: The main function of pre-conventional dendritic cells is to constantly replenish conventional dendritic cells. In response to infection additional pre-cDCs are recruited to the site of infection to meet increased tissue demand, and this pre-DC recruitment may be necessary for effective T cell responses. The molecular signals that direct pre-cDC migration remain poorly understood but recent research suggest that it involves specific chemokine receptors expressed on pre-cDCs.\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/reports/summary_release.md:\n  Line 3136: | GO:0097029 | mature conventional dendritic cell differentiation                                                                       | results in acquisition of features of     | CL:0000841 | mature conventional dendritic cell                           |\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/imports/merged_import.owl:\n  Line 105910: # Class: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0097029> (mature conventional dendritic cell differentiation)\n\n  Line 105912: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"GOC:BHF\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"PMID:15845453\") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0097029> \"The process in which antigen-activated dendritic cells acquire the specialized features of a mature conventional dendritic cell. Mature conventional dendritic cells upregulate the surface expression of MHC molecules, chemokine receptors and adhesion molecules, and increase the number of dendrites (cytoplasmic protrusions) in preparation for migration to lymphoid organs where they present antigen to T cells.\")\n\n  Line 105917: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0097029> \"mature conventional dendritic cell differentiation\")\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/diffs/cl-diff.md:\n  Line 14890: ### pre-conventional dendritic cell `http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002010`\n\n  Line 14892: - [pre-conventional dendritic cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002010) SubClassOf [protein-containing material entity](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PR_000050567) \n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/cl-refactored.owl:\n  Line 3250: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"GOC:add\"^^xsd:string) Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"GOC:amm\"^^xsd:string) Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"GO_REF:0000031\"^^xsd:string) Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"ISBN:0721601464\"^^xsd:string) Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"ISBN:0781735149\"^^xsd:string) Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"PMCID:PMC2346585\"^^xsd:string) Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"PMID:17850486\"^^xsd:string) <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000453> \"Langerhans cell is a conventional dendritic cell that has plasma membrane part CD207. A Langerhans cell is a stellate dendritic cell of myeloid origin, that appears clear on light microscopy and has a dark-staining, indented nucleus and characteristic inclusions (Birbeck granules) in the cytoplasm; Langerhans cells are found principally in the stratum spinosum of the epidermis, but they also occur in other stratified epithelia and have been identified in the lung, lymph nodes, spleen, and thymus.\"^^xsd:string)\n\n  Line 6332: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000840> \"immature conventional dendritic cell\"^^xsd:string)\n\n  Line 6335: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"GOC:add\"^^xsd:string) Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"GOC:tfm\"^^xsd:string) A